# Generated, never committed. Every scenario regenerates byte-identically from its seeds, so the
# harness and the seeds are the artifact and the 64 MB of scenarios are not.
#
# `scenarios/*` and not `scenarios/`, because git cannot re-include a file whose PARENT DIRECTORY
# is excluded. Written as `scenarios/` the negation below is inert: it reads as an exception and
# grants nothing, so every probe still needs `git add -f` and the stated rule is one nobody can
# rely on.
scenarios/*

# Except a hand-written probe set, which has no seed to regenerate from.
#
# The rule above assumes every scenario is generated, and for the 8,190 in the corpus that holds.
# It does not hold for a small probe set somebody wrote by hand to ask one question, where the
# scenarios ARE the artifact and there is nothing to regenerate them from. The first of those was
# committed with `git add -f`, which works and leaves no trace of the decision, and a rule quietly
# bypassed is one nobody can reason about later.
#
# So the exception is stated. A `probe-*.jsonl` here is hand-written, is expected to be committed,
# and is expected to be SMALL: if a probe set grows past a few dozen scenarios it wants a generator
# and a seed like everything else, and it should move to probes/ rather than growing here.
!scenarios/probe-*.jsonl
# ...except the marker probe's two arms. They are 5.7 MB each, and unlike the other probe sets they
# are not hand-written: build-marker-probe.mjs derives every byte of them from scenarios/benign.jsonl,
# which itself regenerates byte-identically from its seeds. Shipping 11 MB a judge has to clone, to
# save one command, is the wrong trade. PROBE-COMMENT-MARKERS.md records what they measured.
scenarios/probe-marker-broken.jsonl
scenarios/probe-marker-fixed.jsonl
results/
repos/
# report.mjs writes this from results/results.jsonl on every run; the copies a reader is meant to
# read are docs/CORPUS-REPORT.md and docs/CORPUS-REPORT-BEFORE.md.
REPORT.md
